---
title: "Layout"
description: "Display, position, overflow, and stacking."
section: Utilities
sectionOrder: 3
order: 1
---

Where an element sits and how it clips. `--display` also carries the flex shorthands (`flex-col`, `inline-flex-row`) that set the direction with the display, so there is no separate direction utility.

`--anchor-name` and `--position-anchor` take states and breakpoints, so the anchor can move with interaction: put `--anchor-name--hover: --block` on every block of an editor, and a toolbar with `--position: absolute; --position-anchor: --block` follows the hovered one. `--position-area` places it: `--position-area: block-start span-inline-end` puts the toolbar above the block, aligned to its start. For finer placement write `anchor()` in plain CSS. The inset utilities take numbers, lengths, and (on the four sides) `auto`, nothing else, so `--top: anchor(bottom)` computes to `0`.

`--top`, `--right`, `--bottom`, and `--left` take `auto` at any breakpoint, which lets a side you set at one width go back to `auto` at another. A bar that sticks to the bottom of a phone screen and to the top of a desktop one is `--position: sticky; --bottom: 0; --bottom--lg: auto; --top: auto; --top--lg: 0`. `--inset`, `--inset-x`, and `--inset-y` take lengths only.

